            <ArticleTitle>Osteolytic lesions: osteitis fibrosa cystica in the setting of severe primary hyperparathyroidism.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr-2017-220603</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2017-220603</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 58-year-old female patient presented with several weeks history of significant bilateral knee pain. Initial knee radiographs demonstrated lucencies of the bony cortex while extensive osteolytic lesions on a routine chest radiograph were suggestive of multiple myeloma or bony metastases. Biochemical investigation revealed primary hyperparathyroidism with renal insufficiency. A parathyroid adenoma was demonstrated on a neck ultrasound and sestamibi scan and subsequently confirmed by histology. We illustrate a case of primary hyperparathyroidism with osteitis fibrosa cystica and brown tumours which were initially mistaken for malignant disease.</AbstractText>
